# Sanity Image URL Builder

Sanity images MUST be served through the Sanity image CDN URL builder combined with `next/image`.

## Setup

```typescript
// lib/sanity/image.ts
import imageUrlBuilder from '@sanity/image-url'
import { client } from './client'

const builder = imageUrlBuilder(client)

export function urlForImage(source: any) {
  return builder.image(source)
}
```

## Usage with next/image

```tsx
import Image from 'next/image'
import { urlForImage } from '@/lib/sanity/image'

// ✅ CORRECT - Sanity CDN + next/image
<Image
  src={urlForImage(article.coverImage)
    .width(800)
    .height(600)
    .format('webp')
    .url()}
  alt={article.coverImage.alt || article.title}
  width={800}
  height={600}
  sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 800px"
/>

// ❌ WRONG - Direct Sanity URL without builder
<Image
  src={article.coverImage.asset.url}
  alt={article.title}
  width={800}
  height={600}
/>

// ❌ WRONG - Not using next/image at all
<img src={urlForImage(article.coverImage).url()} alt={article.title} />
```

## URL Builder Methods

```typescript
urlForImage(source)
  .width(800)           // Resize width
  .height(600)          // Resize height
  .format('webp')       // Convert to WebP
  .quality(80)          // Compression quality
  .fit('crop')          // Crop mode
  .crop('center')       // Crop position
  .url()                // Generate final URL
```

## next.config.js - Remote Patterns

```javascript
// next.config.js
module.exports = {
  images: {
    remotePatterns: [
      {
        protocol: 'https',
        hostname: 'cdn.sanity.io',
        pathname: '/images/**',
      },
    ],
  },
}
```

## Why Both?

1. **Sanity CDN**: Resizes and converts images at the edge
2. **next/image**: Provides responsive srcset, lazy loading, blur placeholder, and prevents layout shift

Using both together ensures optimal performance on all devices.
